Hi [~accountid:625fb4921046bb0071ddf448] [~accountid:61cc1c959ee70a00680edd89] ,

The external counsel came back to us and it seems that not sharing the data would put Zity at risk of non-compliance with the law (as we have already discussed) and the reseller model could be an option, but with certain drawbacks/risks associated. Please see below for full advice and let me know your thoughts. 



{color:#36b37e}Dear Beatriz,{color}

{color:#36b37e}Hope you are doing well. Please find below our thoughts and comments on the two scenarios proposed to avoid sharing personal data with Zity.{color}

{color:#36b37e}*Scenario 1: Bolt only gives access to Zity when authorities require Zity to report that data*{color}

* {color:#36b37e}+_Proactive or reactive obligation applicable to Zity_+{color}

{color:#36b37e}The Royal Decree states an ongoing/regular data sharing obligation with the authorities to be fulfilled both by Zity (as a car rental/car sharing company) and Bolt (as a digital platform/intermediary). So, it would not be possible for Zity to act on a reactive basis, upon request from the authorities, without incurring in liability. Such ongoing/regular communications shall be made within a period not exceeding 24 hours, from reservation or execution of the contract or, if applicable, its cancellation and/or from the beginning of the services.{color}

* {color:#36b37e}+_Potential risks for Bolt and Zity, in case Zity does not meet such data sharing obligation_+{color}

{color:#36b37e}Lack of fulfilment of the data sharing obligation by Zity:{color}

* {color:#36b37e}May be considered a minor breach, subject to fines ranging from €100 to €600, in case the data sharing obligations are fulfilled out of the period set out in the Royal Decree or in case the information provided is insufficient (e.g., in case Bolt decides not to disclose Bolt users’ personal data to Zity but, conversely, Zity decides to partially fulfil the data sharing obligation e.g., with the information that is contained in their servers and that may serve Zity to share with the authorities some of the required fields described in Annex II of the Royal Decree).{color}

{color:#36b37e} {color}

{color:#36b37e}Also, Bolt may face a similar fine, if Bolt does not share with the authorities all the information required in Annex II of the Royal Decree, in case Zity does not agree to facilitate some of the data (e.g., chassis number).{color}

{color:#36b37e} {color}

* {color:#36b37e}May entail a risk for Zity of being fined up to €30,000, for a serious infringement, in case Zity does not fulfil, even partially, the data sharing obligation with the authorities or the internal record keeping obligation.{color}

{color:#36b37e}Even if contractually Bolt decides to cover any potential liability/fines that Zity may receive, Zity’s collaboration to disclose all the relevant details of Annex II to Bolt would be required. Please bear in mind that Bolt could significantly increase the risk profile from a data protection standpoint, given that, acting as a mere intermediary, it would be more difficult to justify the reasoning about collecting/storing certain categories of personal data that may not be relevant for Bolt operations. As a mere example, authorities have apparently accepted that e.g., travel agencies are not required to communicate all the categories of personal data included in Annex 2, but only those that they usually collect when a reservation takes place.{color}

{color:#36b37e}A transitional/grace period (until January 31{color}{color:#36b37e}^st^{color}{color:#36b37e} 2024) has been provided by Spanish authorities -without imposing fines- to adapt the operations/internal systems of the obliged entities to the online platform set out by the Ministry of Interior. However, relying upon such alternative for a soft launch, reducing the potential risks, would not be an option for Zity/Bolt considering that the launch is foreseen in Q1 2024.{color}

{color:#36b37e}+*Scenario 2:*+{color}{color:#36b37e} {color}{color:#36b37e}+*Bolt operates a car sharing service directly using Zity’s vehicles*+{color}

{color:#36b37e}Our understanding is that this model may work to justify that no personal data should be shared with Zity. However, a series of legal and commercial issues should be considered by Bolt:{color}

# {color:#36b37e}Our understanding is that the vehicles would not be exclusively used by Bolt -e.g., Zity would offer them at the same time in their platform or third-party platforms; the vehicles still would have Zity’s logo on them; no fix number of vehicles allocated to Bolt (e.g., 50 cars)-. Also, Zity would still run the whole system required to operate the vehicles -including any support required- and the relevant maintenance/technical assistance. Also, in case of an accident, it may be difficult to define the entity that shall cover the damages/be responsible for the relevant insurance. These factual elements may distort the contractual arrangements and thus might be used by authorities to challenge the fact that Bolt is the one operating the car sharing service.{color}
# {color:#36b37e}Royal Decree indicates that digital platforms are obliged entities "{color}{color:#36b37e}_whether or not they provide the underlying service_{color}{color:#36b37e}". We consider that the relevant article intends to cover scenarios where the digital platform establishes some of the parameters of the services and/or the prices of the service are fixed by the platform, as opposed to a scenario where clearly it is stated in the relevant T&Cs that the service is provided by Bolt that should fall within the category of car sharing/rental company. However, we cannot totally rule out that authorities might construe that Bolt falls within the category of intermediary service and not in the car sharing/rental one, depending on their interpretation of the contractual arrangements and the operation of the service previously flagged. Ideally, either Bolt or Zity should file a consultation to the authorities to check:{color}
## {color:#36b37e}If Bolt is considered directly a car sharing company or still an intermediary, considering that Bolt offers/runs the car sharing services, relying upon vehicles that are already operated by Zity and offered independently in their platform; and{color}
## {color:#36b37e}in case that Bolt and Zity were both considered car sharing companies -e.g., because Zity is still the ‘owner’ of the vehicles; the cars contain their logo; and the vehicles are at Bolt’s and other 3{color}{color:#36b37e}^rd^{color}{color:#36b37e} parties disposal-, if the fulfilment by Bolt of the data sharing obligation is sufficient to meet the requirements of the Royal Decree that may be applicable for both entities.{color}

{color:#36b37e}The idea would be to mitigate as much as possible the risks and to ensure Zity about the feasibility of the intended structure.{color}

# {color:#36b37e}Zity could ask for a contractual guarantee not only covering operational risks but also those related to the data sharing obligation with authorities, among others, for the reasons flagged in 1 and the room for interpreting the Royal Decree under 2 -except if a positive answer from authorities stating that Bolt is the only entity obliged is received-.{color}
# {color:#36b37e}Bolt should determine the group’s company operating the service that should act as data controller and as the entity responsible to fulfil the data sharing obligations. This may have an impact on the existing intra-group agreements.{color}
# {color:#36b37e}Although you are probably aware of, we have detected that FREENOW is already offering Zity services in the FREENOW app/platform. The wording of FREENOW privacy policy and the UX leads us to conclude that the app shares information with Zity to allow them to comply with its data sharing obligations under the Royal Decree, even if this is not expressly mentioned. The business structure already implemented by FREENOW and Zity might also negatively impact the interpretation by authorities of the contractual arrangements that Zity and Bolt may execute.{color}

{color:#36b37e}In any event, we would recommend carrying out an in-depth analysis, if Bolt decides to explore this scenario, to reach a conclusion on the potential adjustments required for its feasibility, to analyse the requirements to be fulfilled by Bolt to directly provide the service (e.g., insurance, change of corporate purpose, land transportation regulations, etc.) and to define, in detail, the contractual arrangements/operational aspects.{color}

{color:#36b37e}+*Conclusions*+{color}

{color:#36b37e}In our view, both scenarios would entail risks for Zity and, thus, our understanding is that they may not be very open to agree on scenario 2. Particularly, if they do not secure a contractual guarantee from Bolt. In any event, this would very much depend on the commercial commitments that Bolt may offer/assume. Also, it should be noted that, from a Bolt internal standpoint, scenario 2 could increase the risk profile not only from a data protection standpoint but around the obligations that Bolt shall assume -in contrast to the role of an intermediary-.{color}

{color:#36b37e}Another alternative, although not ideal, would be for Bolt to explore the possibility to disclose personal data to Zity over Bolt’s customers and to try to define a contractual structure by means of which Zity accepts to pay a commission to Bolt, during a certain period, in case a Bolt user creates, after the initial trip or subsequent ones, an account at Zity.{color}

{color:#36b37e}The new business model under scenarios 1 or 2 would clearly require performing various actions to adapt data protection documentation/clauses/policies at Bolt, as it would be necessary to cover these new data processing activities, including the transfer -or not- of the personal data to Zity and the data sharing with authorities.{color}

{color:#36b37e}We would like to flag that the previous conclusions may vary in the short/medium term, given that the Royal Decree has been repeatedly criticised at a national and an EU level by the potential duplicity of obligations -data sharing over the same personal data to be met both by the intermediary and the car rental company- and also considering that the provisions of the Royal Decree might be against GDPR and Directive 2016/680. This might also reduce, for the time being, the enforcement appetite of the Spanish authorities, until they are certain that the Royal Decree is not formally ‘challenged’ by stakeholders from the industry.{color}

{color:#36b37e}On a separate note, please note that for the purposes of this advice, we did not evaluate potential marketing structures that might lead to construe that Zity is the only obliged entity in case Bolt finally evaluates the possibility to share data with Zity.{color}

{color:#36b37e}At your disposal for any clarification and/or for discussing in more detail any of the above referred points.{color}

[^Bolt Mail - RE_ ES - Drive - Data sharing question - [Legally privileged - Client_Attorney communication] [KWM-LIVE.FID457551].pdf]

